<chapter xml:id="ch-theoretical-foundation">
  <title>Theoretical Foundation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The project needs a theoretical language that can describe mathematical
      justification without treating a short written response as a complete record of
      a student's knowledge. It draws on the cK¢ framework, which describes
      mathematical knowing as situated in particular problems, actions,
      representations, and forms of control.
    </p>

    <p>
      The framework supplies the project's account of <term>control</term>: the means by
      which a person selects an action, evaluates a result, validates a claim, or decides
      that a problem has been resolved. The project then adapts this idea to the limited
      evidence available in short written responses. It analyzes visible traces of
      justification while preserving a strict boundary between what the response shows
      and what a researcher might infer about the student.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter introduces that theoretical foundation and states the claims it can
      support. It does not define the project's response-level coding unit or prescribe
      coding decisions. Those tasks belong to
      <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/> and
      <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-ck-cent-framework">
    <title>The cK¢ Framework</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-ck-cent-situated-knowing">
      <title>Situated Mathematical Knowing</title>

      <p>
        The cK¢ framework treats mathematical knowing as situated rather than as a
        fixed collection of beliefs that can be read directly from a student's words.
        A conception describes a form of mathematical activity that functions within a
        particular range of problems and conditions. The problems posed, the actions
        available, the representations in use, and the criteria for judging progress
        all contribute to that activity.
      </p>

      <p>
        This perspective imposes an important evidentiary caution. A written response
        gives researchers access to what the student wrote in one situation. It does not
        provide direct access to every idea the student considered, every operation the
        student performed, or every criterion the student could use in a different
        situation. The same student may act differently when the question, notation,
        representation, or available information changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project therefore avoids treating one response as a stable description of
        the student. Its immediate object is the mathematical reasoning made visible in
        that response. Broader interpretations require evidence across additional tasks,
        contexts, or sources.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-ck-cent-conception">
      <title>The Conception Quadruplet</title>

      <p>
        In the cK¢ framework, a conception is represented by the quadruplet
        <md>
          (P,R,L,\Sigma).
        </md>
        Each component describes a different aspect of mathematical activity.
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>Problems: <m>P</m></title>
            <p>
              <m>P</m> is the class of problems or situations for which the conception
              functions. A form of reasoning that is productive for one class of problems
              may be incomplete or inappropriate for another.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Operators: <m>R</m></title>
            <p>
              <m>R</m> is the set of mathematical actions used to work on a problem or
              transform a representation in <m>L</m>. Examples include row reducing a
              matrix, substituting into an equation, differentiating a function, and
              rearranging a symbolic expression.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Representation system: <m>L</m></title>
            <p>
              <m>L</m> is the linguistic, symbolic, graphical, or other system through
              which mathematical objects, actions, feedback, and results are expressed.
              Examples include equations, matrices, diagrams, written mathematical
              language, and software interfaces.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Control structure: <m>\Sigma</m></title>
            <p>
              <m>\Sigma</m> is the means used to make choices, evaluate actions, validate
              results, and decide whether a problem has been solved. Examples include
              applying a definition, checking a computation, recognizing that a system is
              consistent, and using the presence of a free variable to classify the number
              of solutions.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        In the system, an operator acts on a representation within a problem situation, 
        and a control structure helps determine which actions and results are acceptable.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project does not attempt to reconstruct this entire quadruplet from one
        response. The quadruplet instead clarifies why the response must be interpreted
        within its question context and why evidence about control is only one part of a
        broader conception.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-ck-cent-controls">
      <title>Control Structures</title>

      <p>
        This project focuses on evidence related to control structures because short
        justifications often reveal how a student evaluates a mathematical situation.
        A response may identify a feature, invoke a rule or definition, and use it to
        support a conclusion. For example, a student may reason from the presence of a
        free variable, the number of pivots, the consistency of a system, the dimension
        of a space, or the sign of a derivative.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>criterion</term> is a particular condition, feature, or result used in a
        response. A <term>control structure</term> is the broader means by which such
        information supports selection, evaluation, validation, or decision-making.
        The two ideas are related but not interchangeable. The statement
        <q>there is a free variable</q> may serve as a criterion in one response; the
        associated control structure concerns how that criterion is used to classify or
        validate a conclusion.
      </p>

      <p>
        This distinction prevents the analysis from becoming keyword counting. A word
        such as <q>pivot</q>, <q>dimension</q>, or <q>consistent</q> does not identify a
        control structure by itself. Researchers must determine what mathematical role
        the idea plays in the response and whether it is being used to support a
        judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same criterion may also support different judgments in different contexts.
        Its force depends on the mathematical state already established and on the goal
        of the question. Control is therefore interpreted relative to the problem, not
        as a universal association between a phrase and a code.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-ck-cent-operations-controls">
      <title>Operations and Controls</title>

      <p>
        An <term>operation</term> creates, transforms, or computes with a mathematical
        object. A <term>control</term> interprets a result and uses it to guide or justify
        a decision. Compare the following statements.
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Operation</title>
            <p><q>I row reduced the augmented matrix.</q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Control</title>
            <p><q>The reduced matrix has no contradictory row, so the system is consistent.</q></p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The first statement reports an action. The second interprets a feature of the
        result and draws a judgment from it. The distinction concerns the role a claim
        plays, not merely the vocabulary it contains.
      </p>

      <p>
        Operations and controls can interact since the result of an operation
        may become the criterion used in a later decision. For example, row reduction is
        an operation, but the resulting pivot pattern or contradictory row may provide
        evidence used to classify the system.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-framework-adaptation">
    <title>Adaptation to Short Written Responses</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-framework-evidence">
      <title>Evidence Available in a Response</title>

      <p>
        The cK¢ framework describes a conception through a coordinated system of
        problems, operators, representations, and controls. A short written response
        usually reveals only a small portion of that system. It may state a criterion
        and conclusion while omitting the operations that produced the criterion. It may
        report a computation without explaining how the result was evaluated. It may
        also leave a connection implicit because the student assumes that the reader
        will supply it.
      </p>

      <p>
        These omissions are not uniquely interpretable. A missing explanation may mean
        that the student did not know the relevant connection, chose not to write it,
        considered it obvious, misunderstood the prompt, or ran out of time. The response
        alone cannot determine which explanation is correct. Researchers must therefore
        distinguish an absence of written evidence from evidence that the student lacks
        an idea.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project operationalizes the theoretical framework at the level of a
        <term>response-level trace</term>. It identifies local pieces of written
        justification that may provide evidence related to control. The project's
        <term>Episode Model</term> represents these traces by recording a criterion, an
        inferential connection, and a judgment. This representation is a project
        construct motivated by the cK¢ framework; it is not a component of the original
        conception quadruplet.
      </p>

      <p>
        An episode therefore has a deliberately limited interpretation. It records how
        the written response appears to justify a local judgment. It does not describe
        everything the student knew, did, believed, or could have done. The complete
        definition and boundary rules appear in
        <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-framework-claims">
      <title>Permitted and Prohibited Claims</title>

      <p>
        Decision rationales should be based only on what can be directly observed in 
        student's response. Here are some examples of language that is consistent with 
        this approach.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-framework-claim-language">
        <title>Language consistent with the evidence boundary.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="48%"/>
          <col width="52%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Supported response-level claim</cell>
            <cell>Unsupported stronger claim</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <q>The response provides evidence related to a control structure.</q>
            </cell>
            <cell><q>The response gives insight into the student's conception.</q></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <q>The student appears to use the presence of a free variable as a
              criterion in this response.</q>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <q>The student always reasons about solution sets through free
              variables.</q>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>
              <q>The written justification contains an episode connecting this
              criterion to this judgment.</q>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <q>This student must have used AI to generate this response.</q>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>
              <q>Across the sampled responses, this form of justification recurs under
              the stated conditions.</q>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <q>This is a pattern in the student's thought process.</q>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The left column does not prevent researchers from developing broader findings.
        It requires those findings to be built from an appropriate body of evidence.
        Repeated patterns across questions, students, or contexts may support claims
        about the corpus or about the performance of the analytic method. Such claims
        must still specify the sampled situations and should not be presented as direct
        access to an individual's complete conception.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-framework-question-frame">
      <title>Question-Level Analytic Context</title>

      <p>
        A short response is interpreted relative to the exact question it answers. The
        words alone are insufficient because the validity and significance of a
        criterion depend on what the problem establishes and what conclusion the student
        is asked to justify.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before responses to a question are analyzed, the research team must establish a
        <term>question-level analytic context</term> containing at least:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>the exact prompt and any accompanying instructions;</li>
        <li>the mathematical objects, data, and representations supplied;</li>
        <li>the judgment, explanation, or computation requested;</li>
        <li>the mathematical state that may be treated as given;</li>
        <li>a defensible reference analysis or solution;</li>
        <li>the distinct goals that a complete response should address; and</li>
        <li>
          the definitions, results, and forms of reasoning available in the course
          context.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Consider the criterion <q>a free variable exists.</q> If a system is already
        known to be consistent, that criterion can support the judgment that the solution
        is not unique. If consistency has not been established, a free variable does not
        by itself show that any solution exists. The written criterion is the same, but
        its inferential force changes with the question's mathematical state.
      </p>

      <p>
        The question-level context also separates local validity from complete goal
        coverage. A response may contain a sound inference but address only one part of
        the prompt. Conversely, a correct final answer may contain no visible
        justification. The project evaluates these properties separately rather than
        treating final-answer correctness as evidence for a control structure.
      </p>

      <p>
        The codebook and controlled vocabulary are therefore not applied as universal
        dictionaries. They are interpreted through question-specific information that
        identifies relevant criteria, possible judgments, valid connections, and common
        incomplete or incorrect uses. The construction of that information is described
        in <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The cK¢ framework gives the project a situated account of mathematical knowing and
      a language for describing operations, representations, and control. The project
      adopts its emphasis on control while restricting its immediate claims to evidence
      visible in a particular response and interpreted within a particular question.
      The Episode Model provides the next step: a response-level representation of the
      local justification through which that evidence appears.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>
